On 22 Dec 2005 08:54:12 -0800, strycat@gmail.com wrote:

>So I walk into Njezjin's liquor emporium with a huge selection of
>potions.  Should I pick up each potion, find out the price, and then
>#name it "X zorkmids" 

  When you name a potion/wand/spellbook/etc., include the 
original description along with the price.  Otherwise you'll have
lots of different potions named $100.


>or is that just a waste of time?

  No, it's not a waste of time.  It may not be good use of your 
time, though.

  For me, liquor stores are a special case.  Early on I'll 
frequently just close the door and walk away.  Probably for the 
same reason you're asking your question: Price-identifying 
potions is a lot of tedious work for little short term gain.


  Another thing about potions is that monsters will frequently 
identify them for you.  They'll quaff them to help themselves
or throw them at you to harm you.

  Price-identifying unknown potions you have in your inventory
is not a bad idea.  And eventually you will want to price-id
all potions that monsters haven't identified for you, because 
that will help you decide what remaining unknown potions you want
to use other methods of identification on.

  Still, price-identifying a liquor store full of potions isn't
really a bad idea -

  - You're going to have to do it at some point.
  - It's not really risky.  
  - Once you name a potion with its description/price that's 
    pretty much it for the game.
  - Duplicate potions in the shop make the price-identifying go 
    faster.
  - Messages like "You are hit with a potion called 
    'emerald-$100'  You feel confused" will help you get a feel
    for the prices of the various potions.
 

>Same question about the various bookstores?

  Scrolls are similar to potions, except that there are some 
useful scrolls you can id or nearly-id by price.  The scrolls
you can nearly id are good choices to use scrolls of identify
on. (When you get to that.)

  Price-identifying can also help prevent you from using up a 
scroll of scare monster (and having to pay for it.)

  Spellbooks are different in that reading a spellbook doesn't
automatically use it up it like quaffing a potion or reading a 
scroll can.  You just want to be sure you don't get a negative 
effect from trying to read it.  Price-identifying can help there.

  All you need to know before trying to read a spellbook is 
whether it's cursed, and its spell level (which is reflected in 
the spellbook price.)  (Although you can always read a blessed 
spellbook, it's not always easy to determine if a spellbook is 
blessed early on.)

  An uncursed $100 spellbook is probably worth getting and trying
to read.  (Valks may be an exception to this.)

>
>This method of ID'ing stuff quickly becomes tedious and not fun (kinda
>like Sokoban only in a different way).

  You have grasped the essentials of the problem.  Safe 
scroll/potion identification without wasting any is tedious.


  Risky reading/quaffing can get your character killed, for very 
little possible gain.  There is no scroll/potion of insta-win.
There might be a spellbook of insta-in: charm monster.

>
>Is there a better way to ID scrolls and potions?  I know I shouldn't
>just randomly quaff potions and read scrolls.  I've started to just
>wait until a I see potions used by monsters and ID them that way.
>Still it seems rather a poor way.  What am I missing?


  Nothing.  (That's how I play. ;-)


  The most important things to identify are armor and weapons.  
Mostly because you can do so safely without wasting resources.
Price-identifying weapons for enchantment is a skill you probably
want to have.  (Note that non-bonesfile weapons/armor with a 
positive enchantment is guaranteed non-cursed.)

  Testing uncursed shields is less risky/wasteful than trying to
identify the scroll of enchant armor, blessing it with holy
water, and then using it on the first +0 red-eyed shield you 
find.  (The RNG will probably have it just go to +1 anyway.  And 
then you'll find a +1 dwarven shield on the next level.)

  Identifying potions of plain/extra/full healing isn't that 
important because you can usually #pray.  True, you may get in
trouble again before your prayer timeout has expired.  But if
you're getting into to trouble that frequently you'll probably
run out of healing potions.


  For efficiency at some point in the game once you've got holy 
water and many scrolls of identify you can try for mass 
identifications.  (Or you may get lucky and find a spellbook of
identify.)

  - Bless all your scrolls of id
  - load up your inventory with items you want to id
    (Dump known items or items you *don't* want identified.)
  - Start reading.

  The problem here is that price-identifying is a great help in
knowing the order in which to try to identify unknown items.


